1
00:00:00,979 --> 00:00:06,279
Let’s start discussing Access Control Lists

2
00:00:06,279 --> 00:00:12,480
For simplicity, let’s assume each user is
associated with one domain and that all processes

3
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:19,020
ran by the user belong to such domain.
In the example, we have a user A and his/her

4
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:26,679
processes also belong to domain A.
We define one access control list per file.

5
00:00:26,679 --> 00:00:33,140
As the name suggests, it is a list, and it
is scanned in order, stopping at the first

6
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:41,309
entry that matches.
If no rights are found for a given domain,

7
00:00:41,309 --> 00:00:48,190
then we assume such a domain as no rights
associated with it.

8
00:00:48,190 --> 00:00:52,690
The entry can contain the usual permissions
for reading (R) writing (W) and executing

9
00:00:52,690 --> 00:00:56,690
(X) but nothing prevents a system from defining
more rights.

10
00:00:56,690 --> 00:01:02,059
An important aspect here is how we implement
such a system. More concretely, what are the

11
00:01:02,059 --> 00:01:10,270
specific elements we store in each entry?
First, we can distinguish rights for individuals

12
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:15,070
and for groups (the latter are useful when
we want to apply the same rights to a set

13
00:01:15,070 --> 00:01:20,210
of individuals that share some features, e.g.,
students).

14
00:01:20,210 --> 00:01:28,570
Then, we can store rights as pairs <UID,GID>.
In this case, we refer to them as ROLES, since

15
00:01:28,570 --> 00:01:34,340
each set of rights is associated not only
to a user but also to the group such user

16
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:39,520
is representing (and one user could in principle
be associated with multiple groups).

17
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:46,040
In such a case, we could also use wildcards,
to define rights that apply to a user independently

18
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:52,640
of each group (<UID,*>).
An alternative implementation is to store

19
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:58,320
rights on a per-user / per-group basis.

20
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:04,730
Revoking or granting permissions is simple
using access control lists.

21
00:02:04,730 --> 00:02:10,780
Let’s say we want to change the rights associated
with user A so that user A can no longer do

22
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:21,540
anything with file F1.
If we append to the ACL of F1 the pair <A,*>=(none)

23
00:02:21,540 --> 00:02:28,680
then this will be always the first entry matched
for user A, and user A will thus have no rights

24
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:33,629
for file F1.
Notice though we might want to clean and keep

25
00:02:33,629 --> 00:02:37,260
our access control list in order, to prevent
it from ever-growing.

